# Impact

An attacker can poison the shared cache with arbitrary HTML or JavaScript.

Any user receiving the poisoned cache entry executes attacker-controlled JavaScript without interacting with malicious links or submitting any input.

Potential consequences include:

* Session hijacking
* Cookie theft (where cookies are accessible)
* Phishing
* DOM manipulation
* Account compromise
* Malware delivery
* Persistent client-side attacks until cache expiration

